# Plan — NLS SES / BA degree completion

## Inspection
- data/nls_ses_processed.csv: 8773 rows x 8 cols (plus int index).
- Types: CASE ID int64; SAMPLE_RACE str (White 5337/Black 2113/Hispanic 1323);
  SAMPLE_SEX str (F 4437/M 4336); FAMILY SIZE float (mean 4.36,sd 2.23,1-12);
  ABILITY: COMPOSITE OF ASVAB SCORE float (mean 1947,sd 346,1016-3068);
  BA DEGREE COMPLETED bool; PERCENTILE IN CLASS float (mean 47.2,sd 25.6);
  SES float (mean -0.009,sd 0.74,-2.45..2.72, z-scored).
- Missing: 0 in all columns.
- Outcome balance: 236/8773 = 2.69% =1 (rare).
- BA by race: White 3.54%, Hispanic 1.44%, Black 1.33%. By sex: F 2.95%, M 2.42%.
- r with BA: ability .181, percentile .168, famsize -.139, SES .061.
- Collinearity: ability-SES .46, percentile-SES .30, ability-famsize -.24.

## Approach
- Academic characteristics = ABILITY + PERCENTILE IN CLASS (curriculum absent from CSV -> excluded).
- Primary model: logistic regression; report raw log-odds beta, OR=exp(beta), standardized beta.
- Robustness: OLS LPM + standardized OLS beta.
- Model A: BA ~ X ; Model B: BA ~ X + SES + ABILITY + PERCENTILE, for X in
  {famsize, Black, Hispanic, male, and checks SES/ability/percentile}.
- Track magnitude change + significance (Wald z/t, p<.05, 95% CI, A->B ratio) and VIFs.
- Target: X whose effect shrinks ~5x and becomes insignificant.

## Preliminary (to confirm)
- Race is the standout: Black -0.876(p<.001)->+0.136(p=.54); Hispanic -0.722(p=.003)->-0.284(p=.26).
  Both become n.s. after adjustment; no other social-bg variable does.
- 0.5024/0.0923 not reproduced in this subset under any scaling -> assumed from original full-NLS analysis.

## Assumptions / uncertainties
- Coefficient type of 0.5024/0.0923 unknown (OR vs standardized vs log-odds); report all.
- Rare outcome (2.69%) affects SE/significance.
- Moderate collinearity ability-SES (.46).
- SES z-scored, percentile 0-100, ability summed z-scores -> standardize for comparability.
- No missingness. Race ref = White.
